Since your tests do not modify server-side state, you should use a single shared account for all tests. This approach is appropriate when you can imagine all tests running at the same time with the same account without affecting each other.

To set this up:

1.  **Create an authentication setup file:** Create `tests/auth.setup.ts` to prepare the authenticated browser state.
2.  **Perform authentication:** In this file, perform your login steps (e.g., navigating to the login page, filling in credentials, and clicking sign-in).
3.  **Save the state:** Use `page.context().storageState({ path: authFile })` to save the authenticated state to a file. It is recommended to store this in a `playwright/.auth` directory and add that directory to your `.gitignore`.
4.  **Reuse the state:** Your tests will then load this existing authenticated state, eliminating the need to authenticate in every test.

Example of the setup file structure:

```typescript
import { test as setup, expect } from '@playwright/test';
import path from 'path';

const authFile = path.join(__dirname, '../playwright/.auth/user.json');

setup('authenticate', async ({ page }) => {
  // Perform authentication steps
  await page.goto('https://github.com/login');
  await page.getByLabel('Username or email address').fill('username');
  await page.getByLabel('Password').fill('password');
  await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Sign in' }).click();

  // Wait for authentication to complete
  await page.waitForURL('https://github.com/');

  // Save the state
  await page.context().storageState({ path: authFile });
});
```

Once configured, your tests will start already authenticated. Note that you must delete the stored state when it expires. If you do not need to keep the state between test runs, you can write the browser state under `testProject.outputDir`, which is automatically cleaned up before every test run.

