llm_response:
Yes, it is possible to mock the entire WebSocket communication without connecting to a real server.

By default, when you use `page.routeWebSocket`, the WebSocket does not connect to the server, allowing you to mock the communication entirely. You can use `webSocketRoute.onMessage` to handle incoming messages and `webSocketRoute.send` to send responses back to the page.

**Example of mocking communication:**
```javascript
await page.routeWebSocket('wss://example.com/ws', ws => {
  ws.onMessage(message => {
    if (message === 'request') {
      ws.send('response');
    }
  });
});
```

If you instead decide you want to connect to the actual server, you can use `webSocketRoute.connectToServer()` to establish the connection and then intercept, modify, or block messages as needed.

